Here are the retrieved knowledge base entries (ranked by similarity):

Entry 1:
  ID          : KB-SQL-004
  Layer       : 1
  Category    : SQL Optimization → Subquery Optimization
  Similarity  : 0.6684
  Title       : Replace correlated subquery with JOIN or CTE
  Problem     : Correlated subqueries execute once for every row in the outer query, causing O(n) repeated scans of the inner table and severely degrading performance at scale.
  Strategy    : Rewrite correlated subqueries as JOIN operations or Common Table Expressions (CTEs) that execute once and are joined back to the outer query.
  Conditions  : []
---
Entry 2:
  ID          : KB-SQL-001
  Layer       : 1
  Category    : SQL Optimization → Column Projection
  Similarity  : 0.5485
  Title       : Avoid SELECT * in large table queries
  Problem     : Using SELECT * retrieves all columns from a table regardless of downstream need, causing unnecessary I/O, increased memory consumption, and preventing columnar storage optimizations such as column pruning.
  Strategy    : Replace SELECT * with an explicit list of only the columns required by the downstream consumer or transformation step.
  Conditions  : []
---
Entry 3:
  ID          : KB-SQL-003
  Layer       : 1
  Category    : SQL Optimization → Predicate Pushdown
  Similarity  : 0.4225
  Title       : Apply predicate pushdown to filter data early in execution
  Problem     : Filters applied after joins or aggregations process more rows than necessary, increasing memory usage, shuffle size, and overall query execution time.
  Strategy    : Rewrite queries to apply WHERE filters as early as possible — before joins, subqueries, or aggregations — so that fewer rows flow into expensive operations.
  Conditions  : []
---

