2026-08-12 20:23:55,726 [INFO] api.main — [Pipeline result]: RecommendationOutput(report_summary='1 inefficiency pattern(s) detected. 3 optimization recommendation(s) generated. Report generated in fallback mode.', total_issues_detected=1, recommendations=[{'rank': 1, 'entry_id': 'KB-SQL-004', 'title': 'Replace correlated subquery with JOIN or CTE', 'severity': 'Critical', 'problem': 'Correlated subqueries execute once for every row in the outer query, causing O(n) repeated scans of the inner table and severely degrading performance at scale.', 'solution': 'Rewrite correlated subqueries as JOIN operations or Common Table Expressions (CTEs) that execute once and are joined back to the outer query.', 'before_example': 'SELECT order_id, amount FROM orders o WHERE amount > (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = o.customer_id);', 'after_example': 'WITH customer_avg AS (SELECT customer_id, AVG(amount) AS avg_amount FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT o.order_id, o.amount FROM orders o JOIN customer_avg ca ON o.customer_id = ca.customer_id WHERE o.amount > ca.avg_amount;', 'explanation': 'The correlated subquery computes AVG(amount) for every single row in the outer query — if orders has 10 million rows, the subquery runs 10 million times. The CTE-based rewrite computes the average once per customer_id group and joins the result back, reducing the operation from O(n) repeated scans to a single aggregation pass plus one join.', 'expected_impact': {'execution_time': '50–95% reduction for large tables — scales linearly with row count', 'cost': 'Very high — eliminates repeated full or partial table scans', 'memory': 'Moderate increase for CTE materialization; net strongly positive'}, 'source': 'SQL Query Optimization in Relational Database Systems — Das, 2020', 'confidence': 0.96}, {'rank': 2, 'entry_id': 'KB-SQL-003', 'title': 'Apply predicate pushdown to filter data early in execution', 'severity': 'High', 'problem': 'Filters applied after joins or aggregations process more rows than necessary, increasing memory usage, shuffle size, and overall query execution time.', 'solution': 'Rewrite queries to apply WHERE filters as early as possible — before joins, subqueries, or aggregations — so that fewer rows flow into expensive operations.', 'before_example': "SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id GROUP BY a.customer_id HAVING a.country = 'IN';", 'after_example': "SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id WHERE a.country = 'IN' GROUP BY a.customer_id;", 'explanation': 'Moving the filter on country from HAVING to WHERE allows the query optimizer to apply it before the join, significantly reducing the number of rows that enter the join and aggregation stages. Most modern optimizers (Spark Catalyst, PostgreSQL planner) will push predicates automatically, but explicitly placing filters early makes the intent clear and guarantees correct behavior in cases where the optimizer cannot infer pushdown safely.', 'expected_impact': {'execution_time': '20–60% reduction depending on filter selectivity', 'cost': 'Medium to high — fewer rows shuffled across join and aggregation stages', 'memory': 'Proportional reduction in intermediate dataset size'}, 'source': 'Google BigQuery SQL Best Practices Documentation', 'confidence': 0.94}, {'rank': 3, 'entry_id': 'L2-CHUNK-0003', 'title': 'L2-CHUNK-0003', 'severity': 'N/A', 'problem': "to ensure that each query has enough capacity to progress.\n\nAccess to more slots doesn't always result in faster performance for a query.\nHowever, a larger pool of slots can improve the performance of", 'solution': 'N/A', 'before_example': 'N/A', 'after_example': 'N/A', 'explanation': 'N/A', 'expected_impact': {}, 'source': 'bigquery_sql_best_practices.txt', 'confidence': 'N/A'}], additional_notes='This report was generated using fallback mode. LLM narrative generation was unavaila