[KB-SQL-004]
Title              : Replace correlated subquery with JOIN or CTE
Category           : SQL Optimization → Subquery Optimization
Severity           : Critical
Problem            : Correlated subqueries execute once for every row in the outer query, causing O(n) repeated scans of the inner table and severely degrading performance at scale.
Optimization       : Rewrite correlated subqueries as JOIN operations or Common Table Expressions (CTEs) that execute once and are joined back to the outer query.
Conditions         : []
Expected Impact    : {'execution_time': '50–95% reduction for large tables — scales linearly with row count', 'cost': 'Very high — eliminates repeated full or partial table scans', 'memory': 'Moderate increase for CTE materialization; net strongly positive'}
Confidence Score   : 0.96
Before             : SELECT order_id, amount FROM orders o WHERE amount > (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = o.customer_id);
After              : WITH customer_avg AS (SELECT customer_id, AVG(amount) AS avg_amount FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT o.order_id, o.amount FROM orders o JOIN customer_avg ca ON o.customer_id = ca.customer_id WHERE o.amount > ca.avg_amount;

---
[KB-SQL-003]
Title              : Apply predicate pushdown to filter data early in execution
Category           : SQL Optimization → Predicate Pushdown
Severity           : High
Problem            : Filters applied after joins or aggregations process more rows than necessary, increasing memory usage, shuffle size, and overall query execution time.
Optimization       : Rewrite queries to apply WHERE filters as early as possible — before joins, subqueries, or aggregations — so that fewer rows flow into expensive operations.
Conditions         : []
Expected Impact    : {'execution_time': '20–60% reduction depending on filter selectivity', 'cost': 'Medium to high — fewer rows shuffled across join and aggregation stages', 'memory': 'Proportional reduction in intermediate dataset size'}
Confidence Score   : 0.94
Before             : SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id GROUP BY a.customer_id HAVING a.country = 'IN';
After              : SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id WHERE a.country = 'IN' GROUP BY a.customer_id;

