  Trade-offs   : Review applicability conditions manually in fallback mode.
  Problem      : Filters applied after joins or aggregations process more rows than necessary, increasing memory usage, shuffle size, and overall query execution time.
  Strategy     : Rewrite queries to apply WHERE filters as early as possible — before joins, subqueries, or aggregations — so that fewer rows flow into expensive operations.
  Explanation  : Moving the filter on country from HAVING to WHERE allows the query optimizer to apply it before the join, significantly reducing the number of rows that enter the join and aggregation stages. Most modern optimizers (Spark Catalyst, PostgreSQL planner) will push predicates automatically, but explicitly placing filters early makes the intent clear and guarantees correct behavior in cases where the optimizer cannot infer pushdown safely.
  Before       : SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id GROUP BY a.customer_id HAVING a.country = 'IN';
  After        : SELECT a.customer_id, SUM(b.amount) FROM customers a JOIN transactions b ON a.customer_id = b.customer_id WHERE a.country = 'IN' GROUP BY a.customer_id;
  Impact       : {'execution_time': '20–60% reduction depending on filter selectivity', 'cost': 'Medium to high — fewer rows shuffled across join and aggregation stages', 'memory': 'Proportional reduction in intermediate dataset size'}
  Source       : Google BigQuery SQL Best Practices Documentation
  Confidence   : 0.94
